 ## Overview
-Linear Opacity Interpolation maps scalar volume data values to opacity using linear interpolation between two threshold values [S1, unofficial]. It serves as the foundational opacity mapping primitive in piecewise linear transfer functions, providing a simple, computationally efficient way to define opacity transitions over a specific data range [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its sibling [Piecewise Linear Opacity Mapping with Gradient Scaling](1335-piecewise-linear-opacity-mapping-with-gradient-scaling.md) by its exclusive reliance on scalar value thresholds, ignoring gradient information for opacity determination.
+Linear Opacity Interpolation maps scalar volume data values to opacity using linear interpolation between two threshold values [S1, unofficial]. It serves as the foundational opacity mapping technique within the Piecewise Linear Mapping and Integration family, providing a simple, computationally inexpensive method to define opacity transitions [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Piecewise Linear Opacity Mapping with Gradient Scaling](1335-piecewise-linear-opacity-mapping-with-gradient-scaling.md), which incorporates gradient magnitude to modulate opacity, this method relies solely on the scalar data value [S1, unofficial].
 
